.\" Manpage for hwdash, based on nvtop

.TH hwdash 1 "@TODAY_MANPAGE@" "Version @nvtop_VERSION_MAJOR@.@nvtop_VERSION_MINOR@.@nvtop_VERSION_PATCH@" "hwdash command"

.SH NAME
hwdash \- interactive host and accelerator monitor

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hwdash
\fR[\fB\-hvCfpPris\fR]
\fR[\fB\-d\fR \fIdelay\fR]
\fR[\fB\-c\fR \fIconfig-file\fR]
\fR[\fB\-E\fR \fIseconds\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
hwdash is an ncurses\-based CPU, memory, accelerator and process monitor based on nvtop.

.SH COMMAND\-LINE OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delay =\fIdelay\fR
Delay between updates, in tenths of seconds (\fIdelay\fR * 0.1s).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-color
Monochrome mode.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-freedom\-unit
Use fahrenheit as temperature scale.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-encode\-hide =\fIseconds\fR
Modify the setting for the encode/decode auto-hide timer. Always visible if negative. Default to 30 seconds.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reverse\-abs
Reverse abscissa: switch the plot data order from "old --- recent" (default) to "recent --- old".
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-no\-plot
Show only one bar plot corresponding to the maximum of all GPUs.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.

.SH INTERACTIVE SETUP WINDOW
.TP
You can enter the setup utility by pressing \fBF2\fR to view and modify the following interface options:
.TP
.I General (General Display Settings)
Monochrome mode, \fBinterface update interval\fR, \fBtemperature in Fahrenheit\fR, and \fBreverse chart direction\fR.
.TP
.I Devices (Device Selection)
A combined \fBCPU and RAM\fR toggle for the host metrics chart, and one toggle per detected GPU/NPU to control which devices are monitored.
.TP
.I CPU Display (CPU Display Settings)
One checkbox per optional field of the CPU/RAM detail block: \fBCPU model and core count\fR, \fBCPU frequency\fR, \fBload averages\fR, \fBCPU package power\fR, \fBLenovo CPU fan speed\fR, \fBavailable memory\fR and \fBswap usage\fR. The \fBDevice CPU\fR title, the CPU utilization and the memory usage are never hidden, and the \fBCPU and RAM\fR toggle of the \fIDevices\fR page stays the master switch.
.TP
.I GPU Display (GPU Display Settings)
\fBEncoder/Decoder idle hiding timer\fR, \fBextended GPU information bar\fR, plot line colors, and the \fBGPU Metric Selection\fR checklist. You can select metrics for all GPUs (tri-state) or per-device (max 4 metrics). Available metrics: GPU utilization, GPU memory utilization, encoder rate, decoder rate, temperature, power draw rate, fan speed, GPU clock rate, GPU memory clock rate, effective load rate, and PCIe RX / TX load.
.TP
.I GPU Processes (GPU Process List)
Show/hide process list, \fBhide HWDash process\fR, \fBsort order\fR, \fBsort field\fR, and \fBvisible columns\fR.

.SH HOST CPU AND MEMORY MONITORING
.TP
On \fBLinux\fR and on \fBmacOS\fR, \fBhwdash\fR can display the whole host usage in a single \fBCPU chart\fR, the
way the GPU utilization and the GPU memory share a GPU chart. The chart holds one percentage history
line per enabled metric: the whole host \fBCPU\fR utilization and the whole host \fBmemory\fR
utilization. The whole host CPU is a device of its own, the \fBCPU device\fR, listed before the GPU
devices. The monitoring screen is a stack of sections, from the top of the terminal to the bottom:
the CPU device (its detail block then its chart), one section per GPU device in its index order
(each detail block directly above the chart that draws that device only), the process list, the
unused space and the shortcut line, anchored to the last row. One blank row separates two sections,
never a device detail block from its own chart. The first row of a detail block is the title row of
its device and stays at the column of the section; every row below it starts at the column the vertical
Y axis of the chart of that same device is drawn at, where a narrow terminal truncates it rather than
wrapping it into the next section. No chart ever shares a row with another one and none
takes a column away from another: every visible device owns a full width chart of its own, and all
of the visible charts share the available vertical space equally. The CPU chart is an ordinary
chart: same size rules, same \fB0/25/50/75/100\fR scale, same border, same time axis and same
resize behaviour.
.TP
The legend of a chart is the key of a curve, not a readout of it. All the entries of a chart are
drawn on a single row, horizontally, in plot line order, and every entry carries a short \fB---\fR
swatch: the text and the swatch of an entry use the color of the plot line they stand for, so a GPU
chart reads \fBGPU0 % ---    GPU0 mem% ---\fR and the CPU chart \fBCPU % ---    RAM % ---\fR. A
legend never shows the current value of its metric, which the detail block above the chart already
shows; a row that does not fit a narrow chart is cut, never wrapped.
.TP
The current value of every line of a chart is read out in a \fBgutter\fR at its right edge, in the
color of that line: \fB0.4%\fR, \fB92.6%\fR, \fB100.0%\fR. The frame extends to the column immediately
before the fixed-position value, while the section itself keeps the width the layout gave it. A value
sits on the row its own line ends on; when two values would land on the same
row, or too close to be read apart, they take separate adjacent rows, the highest value highest on the
screen and, on a tie, the lower plot line above the higher one. A readout always stays inside the data
region, below the legend and above the time axis, and a line whose newest sample is not available
shows no readout rather than a made up zero.
.TP
The oldest edge of a chart is kept clean: every curve begins against the Y axis as one connected
segment, and the oldest sample leaves together with its transition, so neither a detached short mark
nor a vertical remnant hangs at the edge. The same connected edge applies with a reversed time axis.
.TP
On a small terminal that cannot give the minimum chart height to every device, whole device
sections are left out, in a fixed order: the \fBCPU device first\fR, then the \fBGPU device sections
from the highest index to the lowest\fR. A GPU section that is left out has neither a detail block
nor a chart, so the visible GPUs stay an ordered prefix of the monitored ones; they are still
monitored, they still keep their history and they are still listed in the setup screen. Everything
comes back as soon as the terminal is tall enough again.
.TP
The CPU utilization is the difference between two successive samples of the aggregate
\fI/proc/stat\fR counters (\fBidle\fR and \fBiowait\fR count as idle time, \fBguest\fR and
\fBguest_nice\fR are excluded to avoid double counting). Memory usage is \fBMemTotal -
MemAvailable\fR read from \fI/proc/meminfo\fR. Data that cannot be read, including
\fBMemAvailable\fR larger than \fBMemTotal\fR, displays \fBN/A\fR in the detail block and leaves a
hole in the curve: it is never plotted as a zero.
.TP
On \fBmacOS\fR the same fields come from the interfaces of that kernel: the utilization from the
cumulative tick counters of each processor of \fIPROCESSOR_CPU_LOAD_INFO\fR (\fBuser\fR, \fBnice\fR
and \fBsystem\fR busy, \fBidle\fR the only idle state), the memory from \fIHOST_VM_INFO64\fR and
\fIhw.memsize\fR as \fB(internal - purgeable) + wired + compressor\fR pages, the load averages from
\fBgetloadavg\fR and the swap from \fIvm.swapusage\fR. The reference is kept per processor and per
sampling state, and restarts - reporting no utilization for that interval - when the processor count
changes or a counter goes backwards. \fBpurgeable\fR is subtracted from the anonymous memory it is a
part of, and the compressed pages are charged at the space they occupy in RAM. That figure is not the
Linux \fBMemAvailable\fR estimate, which the kernel computes with reserves and reclaim heuristics:
both answer how much memory cannot be handed to a new allocation without going through the pager, not
the same number. \fIvm.swapusage\fR either answers a pair - where it answers none allocated the block
reads \fBSWAP 0.00/0.00 GiB\fR - or, on some configurations, nothing at all, which reads \fBSWAP
N/A\fR. A core count the kernel does not answer stays unknown rather than printed as the thread
count. Both lines are disabled by default on every other platform.
.TP
The detail block above the CPU chart reads \fBDevice CPU [model] CORES physicalC/logicalT\fR, then
the CPU utilization, the average CPU frequency, the 1/5/15 minute load averages and the power of the
CPU package in watts, then the memory used/total, the memory percentage, the available memory and
the swap used/total. The model and the core counts come from \fI/proc/cpuinfo\fR and
\fI/sys/devices/system/cpu\fR on Linux, or from \fImachdep.cpu.brand_string\fR (with \fIhw.model\fR
where the brand is not answered), \fIhw.physicalcpu\fR and \fIhw.activecpu\fR on macOS; the frequency
from the \fIcpuinfo_cur_freq\fR and \fIscaling_cur_freq\fR sysfs entries, where a machine that has no
readable one shows \fBFREQ N/A\fR rather than the nominal frequency of the part; the load averages
from \fI/proc/loadavg\fR or, on macOS, from \fBgetloadavg\fR; the swap from
\fI/proc/meminfo\fR and the package power from the standard power interfaces of the kernel: the
powercap (RAPL) zone declaring the package (\fBpackage\fR, \fBpkg\fR, \fBsoc\fR or the AMD \fBamd\fR
domain, found by its \fIname\fR and not at a fixed path), whose \fIenergy_uj\fR delta over the
elapsed monotonic time gives the watts, or, where there is no such zone, an hwmon \fIpower*_input\fR
sensor whose \fIpower*_label\fR says it is the package. No raw MSR access and no external tool are
used, so a machine whose kernel exposes no readable counter to this process shows \fBPOWER N/A\fR:
an energy counter needs two trustworthy samples before it means anything, and a counter reset, a
wrap adding up to an impossible power, a malformed file or a zone that disappears all read \fBN/A\fR
and never a made up \fB0.0W\fR. The titles of the fields are highlighted in the color of the GPU
detail block titles, the values in the normal color. Unavailable data reads \fBN/A\fR. On a narrow
terminal the block drops the swap first, then the 15 and 5 minute load averages, then the available
memory, then the package power, then the frequency and the static CPU information, and truncates an
overlong CPU model: the CPU utilization and the memory used/total/percentage stay as long as the
block fits.
.TP
The two options are saved with \fBF4\fR in the \fB[GeneralOption]\fR section of \fIinterface.ini\fR
as \fBShowHostCpuUsage\fR and \fBShowHostMemUsage\fR.
.TP
The fields of the CPU Display page are saved in the \fB[HostOption]\fR section, one key per
optional field of the detail block: \fBShowCpuModel\fR, \fBShowCpuFreq\fR, \fBShowCpuLoad\fR,
\fBShowCpuPower\fR, \fBShowCpuFan\fR, \fBShowRamAvailable\fR and \fBShowSwap\fR. A key is
\fBtrue\fR when its field is shown and \fBfalse\fR when it is hidden, and all of them default to
\fBtrue\fR; a configuration file without that section keeps every field shown.

.SH INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
.TP
The following commands are available while in hwdash:
.TP
.BR Up
Select (highlight) the previous process.
.TP
.BR Down
Select (highlight) the next process.
.TP
.BR Left\ /\ Right
Scroll in the process row.
.TP
.BR +
Sort increasingly.
.TP
.BR -
Sort decreasingly.
.TP
.BR F2
Enter the setup utility to modify the interface options.
.TP
.BR F4
Save the current interface options to persistent storage.
See the \fBCONFIGURATION FILE\fR section.
.TP
.BR F9
"Kill" process: Select a signal to send to the highlighted process.
.TP
.BR F6
Sort: Select the field for sorting. The current sort field is highlighted inside the header bar.
.TP
.BR F10 ", " q ", " Esc
Quit.

.SH DYNAMIC METERS
.TP
When the video encoder (ENC) and decoder (DEC) of the GPU are in use, new percentage meters will appear next to the GPU utilization bar. They will disappear automatically after some time of inactivity (see option -E).

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.LP
The configuration file follows the \fIXDG Base Directory Specification\fR and is stored at \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hwdash/interface.ini\fR. The location defaults to \fI$HOME/.config/hwdash/interface.ini\fR if the XDG location is not defined.
.LP
Do not edit this file. The file is automatically created or updated upon toggling the interface saving key \fBF4\fR.
.LP
The configuration is loaded during program initialization.
If no configuration file is present, default options are used.

.SH MEMORY SIZES
.TP
Memory sizes in hwdash are displayed as multiples of 1024 bytes or 1 KiB.

.SH BUGS
.TP
.BR "Some fields are shown as N/A"
Ask AMD or NVIDIA for better support of your hardware!

If your card uses the AMDGPU driver, a more recent kernel might have improved support.
.TP
.BR "Compatibility issues"
Does not work with nouveau driver stack and older NVIDIA GPU for the time being.

Does not work for AMD graphics card using the old radeon driver.
.SH AUTHOR
hwdash changes are maintained by etan68 and contributors. The nvtop foundation was written by Maxime Schmitt and nvtop contributors.
